// Original model answers. Use them as language examples, not scripts to memorise.
const topic=(level,type,title,seconds,answer,mark,grammar,vocab,points)=>({level,type,title,seconds,answer,mark,grammar,vocab,points});
export const extraSpeakingTopics=[
topic('A1','Beginner · Family','Who do you live with?',45,'I live with my parents and my sister. Our home is small, but I like it. We eat dinner together in the evening. My sister is funny and kind.','We eat dinner together','Present simple describes routine. With we use eat, not eats.','together — birga; kind — mehribon',['Say who lives with you','Describe one person','Mention a shared activity']),
topic('A1','Beginner · Food','What food do you like?',45,'I like rice and vegetables. My favourite meal is soup with bread. I usually eat it at home. I do not like very spicy food, but I want to try new dishes.','I do not like','Like + noun; do not + base verb for a negative.','spicy — achchiq; dish — taom',['Name a food','Say when you eat it','Mention a preference']),
topic('A1','Beginner · Home','Describe your room.',45,'My room is bright and quiet. There is a desk next to the window. There are two books on the desk. I study there after school, and I keep my bag under the chair.','There is a desk','There is + singular; there are + plural. Next to and under describe position.','bright — yorug‘; next to — yonida',['Describe size or light','Name two objects','Say where they are']),
topic('A1','Beginner · Free time','What do you do at weekends?',45,'On Saturday, I play football with my friends. On Sunday, I help my family and read a book. Sometimes we visit my grandparents. I like weekends because I have more time to relax.','because I have more time','On + day; sometimes describes frequency; because introduces a reason.','relax — dam olmoq; visit — borib ko‘rmoq',['Name an activity','Say who joins you','Explain why you like it']),
topic('A2','Elementary · Travel','How do you get to your classes?',60,'I usually take the bus because my school is quite far from home. The journey takes about twenty minutes. Yesterday I walked with a friend instead. It took longer, but we enjoyed the fresh air.','Yesterday I walked','Present simple for routine; past simple for yesterday. Took is the past form of take.','journey — safar; fresh air — toza havo',['Describe your normal journey','Mention the time','Compare a different journey']),
topic('A2','Elementary · Shopping','Tell me about something you bought recently.',60,'Last week, I bought a small notebook for my English lessons. I chose a green one because it was easy to find in my bag. It was not expensive, and I use it to write new phrases.','I chose a green one','Past simple: bought, chose, was. To + verb explains purpose.','recently — yaqinda; phrase — ibora',['What did you buy?','Why did you choose it?','How do you use it?']),
topic('A2','Elementary · Plans','What would you like to do next summer?',60,'I would like to visit my cousin next summer. She lives near a lake, so we could go swimming. I am going to save some money for the journey. If I cannot travel, we will talk online.','I am going to save','Would like + to; going to for a plan; if + present, will + verb.','save money — pul yig‘moq; cousin — amakivachcha yoki boshqa qarindosh',['Give a plan','Explain why','Mention an alternative']),
topic('A2','Elementary · Directions','How would a visitor find your favourite café?',60,'From the bus stop, walk straight ahead and turn left at the bank. The café is opposite a bookshop. It is easy to recognise because it has a blue door. I like sitting near the window.','turn left at the bank','Imperatives use the base verb: walk, turn. Opposite describes location.','straight ahead — to‘g‘riga; opposite — ro‘parasida',['Give a starting point','Explain two steps','Describe a landmark']),
topic('B1','Intermediate · Friendship','What makes someone a good friend?',75,'For me, a good friend is someone who listens without judging too quickly. We do not have to agree about everything. What matters is being able to speak honestly and ask for help when something goes wrong.','someone who listens','Who introduces a relative clause about a person. Being follows is after what matters.','judge — hukm qilmoq; speak honestly — ochiq gapirmoq',['State an important quality','Explain it','Give a realistic example']),
topic('B1','Intermediate · Experience','Tell me about a time you changed your plans.',75,'I once planned a picnic with some classmates, but it started raining heavily. Instead of cancelling the day, we cooked lunch together at my friend’s house. It turned out better than expected because we had more time to talk.','It turned out better than expected','Past simple narrates completed events. Instead of + -ing; better is a comparative.','turn out — natijada bo‘lib chiqmoq; cancel — bekor qilmoq',['Explain the original plan','Describe the change','Say how you felt afterwards']),
topic('B1','Intermediate · Health','How do you manage stress?',75,'When I feel stressed, I try to work out what is causing it. Sometimes I need a short walk; sometimes I need to divide a large task into smaller steps. Talking to a trusted friend also helps me see the situation more clearly.','divide a large task into smaller steps','When + present for a repeated situation. Need + to; help + object + base verb.','trusted — ishonchli; work out — aniqlamoq',['Describe a common cause','Explain your response','Mention what works for you']),
topic('B1','Intermediate · Community','What would you improve in your neighbourhood?',75,'I would improve the pavements near our school. Some sections are uneven, which makes walking difficult for older people. If the local council repaired them, more people could walk comfortably instead of taking a car for short journeys.','If the local council repaired them','Second conditional: if + past, could + base verb. Which refers to the previous situation.','pavement — piyodalar yo‘li; uneven — notekis',['Choose one improvement','Explain the problem','Describe a benefit']),
topic('B2','IELTS Part 1 · Hometown','Has your hometown changed in recent years?',75,'Yes, it has become much busier. Several new shops have opened near the main square, and public transport is more frequent. I appreciate the convenience, although I miss the quieter streets we used to have when I was younger.','we used to have','Present perfect describes change up to now; used to + verb describes a past state.','frequent — tez-tez; appreciate the convenience — qulaylikni qadrlamoq',['Give a direct answer','Describe a change','Add your opinion']),
topic('B2','IELTS Part 1 · Reading','Do you prefer reading on paper or on a screen?',75,'For a long book, I tend to choose paper because I find it easier to concentrate. For a short article, a screen is more practical, especially when I want to check an unfamiliar word. My preference really depends on the purpose.','depends on the purpose','Tend to + verb describes a tendency. Find it + adjective + to infinitive.','concentrate — diqqatni jamlamoq; unfamiliar — notanish',['Choose or qualify a preference','Explain the reason','Compare a second situation']),
topic('B2','IELTS Part 2 · A helpful person','Describe someone who helped you learn something.',120,'I would like to talk about a colleague who helped me learn to present ideas clearly. At first, I tried to include every detail, so my explanations were difficult to follow. She suggested starting with the main point and using one concrete example. We practised a short presentation together, and she gave me feedback after each attempt. What I valued most was her patience: she let me make mistakes without making me feel embarrassed. I still use that approach whenever I explain a new topic.','What I valued most was her patience','Past simple narrates the experience. What I valued most is a cleft structure; whenever means every time.','concrete example — aniq misol; difficult to follow — tushunish qiyin',['Who the person is','What you were learning','How they helped','Why it mattered']),
topic('B2','IELTS Part 2 · A useful object','Describe an object you use every day.',120,'An object I rely on every day is a small notebook. It is fairly ordinary, with a plain cover and blank pages, but it helps me organise my thoughts. I started using it when I noticed that reminders on my phone were becoming a distraction. Now I write down three priorities each morning and add short notes during the day. It does not solve every problem, of course, but it makes unfinished tasks easier to see. I would probably feel rather disorganised without it.','I rely on every day','Relative clause with omitted that; when + past; would + verb for a hypothetical situation.','rely on — tayanmoq; priorities — ustuvor vazifalar',['What it is','How you got it','How you use it','Why it is useful']),
topic('B2','IELTS Part 2 · A place','Describe a place where you enjoy spending time.',120,'There is a small park near my home where I often go in the early evening. It has a walking path, a few benches and some old trees that provide shade. I first went there with a friend, but now I sometimes visit on my own. What attracts me is the change of pace: after spending hours looking at a screen, walking outside helps me think more clearly. The park is not particularly famous, yet it has become an important part of my routine.','the change of pace','Where introduces a place clause. After + -ing; present perfect links a past change to now.','provide shade — soya bermoq; on my own — yolg‘iz',['Where it is','What it looks like','What you do there','Why you enjoy it']),
topic('B2','IELTS Part 3 · Education','Should schools teach practical skills?',90,'I think practical skills deserve a place in education, provided they complement rather than replace deeper understanding. For instance, learning to manage a simple budget can make mathematical ideas feel relevant. The challenge is choosing skills that remain useful as circumstances change, instead of preparing pupils only for one narrow task.','provided they complement','Provided introduces a condition. Rather than + verb contrasts alternatives.','complement — to‘ldirmoq; narrow task — tor doiradagi vazifa',['State a qualified view','Give an example','Consider a limitation']),
topic('C1','IELTS Part 3 · Cities','How should cities decide which transport projects to fund?',90,'Cities should evaluate projects against clear goals, such as accessibility, reliability and environmental impact. Passenger numbers matter, but they do not reveal who is currently excluded from the network. A route serving a smaller population may still be valuable if it connects people to essential services. I would therefore favour transparent criteria and a review of outcomes after implementation.','who is currently excluded','Embedded question after reveal; may + verb for possibility; would favour expresses a considered preference.','transparent criteria — ochiq mezonlar; accessibility — foydalana olish qulayligi',['Identify criteria','Explain a trade-off','Recommend evaluation']),
topic('C1','IELTS Part 3 · Information','Why can misinformation be difficult to correct?',90,'A correction competes with an explanation people may already find familiar or emotionally satisfying. Simply repeating that a claim is false may leave a gap in their understanding. A more effective response can explain what happened, show how the evidence was obtained and acknowledge uncertainty. Even then, trust in the source will influence whether the correction is accepted.','acknowledge uncertainty','May and can express qualified claims. Whether introduces an embedded yes/no question; passive is accepted.','misinformation — noto‘g‘ri axborot; acknowledge — tan olmoq',['Explain a cause','Suggest an approach','Mention the role of trust']),
topic('C1','IELTS Part 3 · Work','Is working from home equally suitable for everyone?',90,'Its suitability depends on the task, the worker’s circumstances and the support available. Someone doing focused individual work may benefit from fewer interruptions, whereas a new employee might lose informal opportunities to learn from colleagues. Rather than imposing one arrangement universally, organisations could agree on expectations and judge performance through outcomes that employees can reasonably influence.','whereas a new employee might lose','Whereas contrasts cases. Rather than + -ing; relative clause that employees can influence.','informal opportunities — norasmiy imkoniyatlar; suitability — mos kelish',['Give a qualified answer','Compare two cases','Suggest a fair approach']),
topic('C1','IELTS Part 3 · Environment','Can individual action solve environmental problems?',90,'Individual choices can contribute, but their effect is shaped by infrastructure and policy. People cannot reliably choose public transport if no practical service exists. Equally, well-designed services need public participation. I would see individual and collective action as mutually reinforcing, while being cautious about campaigns that place the entire burden on households.','mutually reinforcing','Cannot + base verb expresses a constraint; while + -ing adds a qualification.','infrastructure — infratuzilma; mutually reinforcing — bir-birini kuchaytiruvchi',['State your position','Identify a constraint','Explain the relationship'])
];
